A1. A cat's siesta. ~ NAP
A4. Possessive pronoun. ~ ITS
A7. Doctors of Medicine (abbr.). ~ MDS
A9. A woman's weapon of defense or attack. ~ JAW
A12. An image of a divinity. ~ IDOL
A14. A sore. ~ ULCER
A16. The goddess of the moon. ~ LUNA
A17. What colored boys get when a ghost pays them a visit. ~ PALE
A18. These scrap with the bulls over who gets the lambskin. ~ BEARS
A19. There are two of these on a rattlesnake. ~ ENDS
A20. Never go in swimming after one of these. (You won't find it there.) ~ MEAL
A22. Employ. ~ USE
A23. The shade of Uncle Tom. ~ INKY
A25. The land of God's frozen people. ~ NORTHLAND
A27. What men use when they want mail-order wives (pl. abbr.). ~ ADS
A30. A Yapp exclamation. ~ HEY
A31. A Scottish understanding. ~ KEN
A33. What girls take when they say they will meet you in an hour. ~ LONGER
A36. A son of Erin. (Starts with "O."). ~ OBRIAN
A38. What snow shovels get in the winter and lawn mowers get in the summer. (Ask any suburbanite.) ~ LOANED
A39. These are a matter of geography and bankrolls. ~ MORALS
A40. The face on the barroom floor has been superseded by this sign on the barroom door. ~ CLOSED
A42. What a merry mamma gives a sleepy sheik. ~ MITTEN
A44. What Cleopatra used to do her disappearing act. ~ ASP
A45. An habitual inebriate. ~ SOP
A47. If you got 56 vertical this one won't be hard. ~ SLY
A48. What little drops of liquor do. (Not explode.) ~ STIMULATE
A50. Norwegian "Straight-eights." ~ SKIS
A52. A son of Abijah, and third King of Judah (Bib.). ~ ASA
A53. A baby whirlpool. ~ EDDY
A56. A politician's chance of going to heaven. ~ SLIM
A57. A battleground. (This has nothing to do with domestic mêlées.) ~ ARENA
A59. What a dry country is if it's really dry. ~ ARID
A61. What people eventually sustain from following the ponies. ~ LOSS
A62. Scatter. ~ STREW
A63. What one does when out of spirits. (Don't ask Mr. Volstead.) ~ MOPE
A64. A well-known reply. ~ YES
A65. This is blue on nice days. ~ SKY
A66. No movie is complete without this. ~ SEX
A67. Something used for straightening crooks. ~ PEN

D1. A pinch. ~ NIP
D2. The first person to get a loudspeaker. ~ ADAM
D3. A very powerful kind of cat. ~ POLE
D5. A Saturday night fixture. ~ TUB
D6. A gum shoe. ~ SLEUTH
D7. Simply. ~ MERELY
D8. Pill shooters (pl. abbr.). ~ DRS
D9. What a second-hand car is often sold for. ~ JUNK
D10. First name of the citizen who "wears no man's collar." ~ ANDY
D11. Used to be. ~ WAS
D13. Absence of avoirdupois. ~ LEAN
D15. What an American Indian does with a government check. ~ CASHES
D16. Money talks; but when you do this with it, it says good-by. ~ LEND
D21. Abodes of newlyweds. ~ LOVENESTS
D23. A pleasant state to be in. ~ INEBRIATE
D24. The kind of a friend who always seems to be in need. ~ PAL
D26. The headquarters of modern highway robbers. ~ INN
D28. Playthings of boys when they're in kindergarten and in college. ~ DOLLS
D29. What reformers do. ~ SNOOP
D31. A Scotchman's "Kuppenheimers." ~ KILTS
D32. What artists draw on. (Not a bank.) ~ EASEL
D34. A bull-thrower's weapon. ~ GAS
D35. You can't paint a town this color with water colors. ~ RED
D36. A Dutch uncle. ~ OOM
D37. Mus decumanus, or in the King's English a muroid or murine rodent. ~ RAT
D40. A midnight soloist. ~ CAT
D41. A cat that does his stuff and earns his bread and butter. ~ MOUSER
D43. Abbreviation for the city sometimes known as New Jerusalem. ~ NYC
D45. Go back to your childhood for this: What a smart guy was called. ~ SMARTY
D46. These are no good on earth (abbr.). ~ PLANES
D48. The well-known man from Seattle. ~ SIMS
D49. A kind of cheese. ~ EDAM
D50. A small bitter plum of the blackthorn tree. ~ SLOE
D51. The finishing touch in the movies. ~ KISS
D54. Aeroplanes are good until they do this. ~ DROP
D55. What dogs do when cans are tied to their tails. ~ YIPE
D56. A characteristic attributed to foxes and pawnbrokers. ~ SLY
D57. Request. ~ ASK
D58. What angry wives inspire. ~ AWE
D60. Where Daniel became famous. ~ DEN


